\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Topic shortcut and scrambled labels.}
Three observations argue against a topic shortcut: unstable topic OOD (F1\,=\,0.541$\pm$0.207), scrambled labels matching correct within 3.9\,pp, and label-free MLM detection (F1\,$\geq$\,0.97 at $K \leq 5$). The scrambled-correct equivalence admits two hypotheses: classification regularizes representations independently of label content, or random permutation over 9 classes preserves enough correlations by chance; distinguishing these requires varying class count with constant domain exposure. Topic classification occasionally approaches MLM on ActorAttack (0.625 vs.\ 0.893), reflecting domain exposure rather than the topic objective; extreme variance (std\,=\,0.141--0.207) confirms initialization sensitivity.

\paragraph{Single-LLM confound and MLM collapse.}
ToxicChat (+18--42\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:human_data}) and cross-LLM experiments (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}) confirm transfer beyond Qwen3-8B. Mixed-LLM training reveals MLM collapse (0.289 $\approx$ vanilla) while 9-class maintains 0.818; one hypothesis is that MLM captures LLM-specific surface patterns while classification extracts style-invariant features.

\paragraph{Variance reversal on human data.}
Classification supervision reduces variance 4--20$\times$ on synthetic OOD; on ToxicChat this reverses (std\,=\,0.087 vs.\ 0.013). Frozen probing localizes MLM instability to Stage-2: frozen MLM outperforms the full pipeline on ActorAttack (0.941 vs.\ 0.893), while 9-class frozen matches (0.972; Appendix~\ref{app:frozen_verify}).

\paragraph{Representation, temporal, and lexical analysis.}
CKA confirms DAPT reshapes representations non-monotonically (Appendix~\ref{sec:cka_heatmap}). Mean pooling matches BiGRU ($|\Delta| \leq 1$\,pp; Section~\ref{sec:decomposition}), confirming per-turn content features drive detection. TF-IDF collapse on ActorAttack (0.261 vs.\ 0.961 on DD) establishes a difficulty threshold; the $\geq$63\,pp gap demonstrates beyond-lexical features. Three encoders (125M--355M) differ in pretraining; same-architecture scaling is needed. Cross-LLM error decomposition confirms F1 degradation is driven by false positives on unfamiliar benign text, not missed attacks (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}).

